            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Hi!\r\n\r\n> I had tried to copy parts of the code for builtin opts, but I don't know how to limit the user input to two characters.\r\n\r\nMy first suggestion is to look in direction of [`MiniJump2d.builtin_opts.single_character`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/305debd01d486f94a9f6ecfec90321ea98dc5dd9/lua/mini/jump2d.lua#L518) (more specifically, [this helper](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/305debd01d486f94a9f6ecfec90321ea98dc5dd9/lua/mini/jump2d.lua#L493)) and ask user twice for an input character. Use `vim.fn.getcharstr` instead of `input_fun` (or copy code for `H.getcharstr()`.\r\n\r\n> I have also found the builtin jump by query option to be great, but it is case-sensitive. Is there a way to make it case-insensitive?\r\n\r\nIt should be possible, although a bit hacky. Again, take a look at [this helper]([this helper](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/305debd01d486f94a9f6ecfec90321ea98dc5dd9/lua/mini/jump2d.lua#L493)). Use `vim.fn.input` instead of `input_fun` and modify its output to model case insensitivity with Lua pattern. Like if output is `ab` then \"case-insensitive\" pattern should be `[aA][bB]`. Use Lua's `string.upper()` and `string.lower()` to achieve it programmatically.\r\n\r\nI am not sure when I'd be able to write a full perfectly working example of `HopChar2` for you, sorry.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Hi!</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I had tried to copy parts of the code for builtin opts, but I don't know how to limit the user input to two characters.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">My first suggestion is to look in direction of <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/305debd01d486f94a9f6ecfec90321ea98dc5dd9/lua/mini/jump2d.lua#L518\"><code class=\"notranslate\">MiniJump2d.builtin_opts.single_character</code></a> (more specifically, <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/305debd01d486f94a9f6ecfec90321ea98dc5dd9/lua/mini/jump2d.lua#L493\">this helper</a>) and ask user twice for an input character. Use <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.fn.getcharstr</code> instead of <code class=\"notranslate\">input_fun</code> (or copy code for <code class=\"notranslate\">H.getcharstr()</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I have also found the builtin jump by query option to be great, but it is case-sensitive. Is there a way to make it case-insensitive?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">It should be possible, although a bit hacky. Again, take a look at [this helper](<a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/305debd01d486f94a9f6ecfec90321ea98dc5dd9/lua/mini/jump2d.lua#L493\">this helper</a>). Use <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.fn.input</code> instead of <code class=\"notranslate\">input_fun</code> and modify its output to model case insensitivity with Lua pattern. Like if output is <code class=\"notranslate\">ab</code> then \"case-insensitive\" pattern should be <code class=\"notranslate\">[aA][bB]</code>. Use Lua's <code class=\"notranslate\">string.upper()</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">string.lower()</code> to achieve it programmatically.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I am not sure when I'd be able to write a full perfectly working example of <code class=\"notranslate\">HopChar2</code> for you, sorry.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Hi!\n\nI had tried to copy parts of the code for builtin opts, but I don't know how to limit the user input to two characters.\n\nMy first suggestion is to look in direction of MiniJump2d.builtin_opts.single_character (more specifically, this helper) and ask user twice for an input character. Use vim.fn.getcharstr instead of input_fun (or copy code for H.getcharstr().\n\nI have also found the builtin jump by query option to be great, but it is case-sensitive. Is there a way to make it case-insensitive?\n\nIt should be possible, although a bit hacky. Again, take a look at [this helper](this helper). Use vim.fn.input instead of input_fun and modify its output to model case insensitivity with Lua pattern. Like if output is ab then \"case-insensitive\" pattern should be [aA][bB]. Use Lua's string.upper() and string.lower() to achieve it programmatically.\nI am not sure when I'd be able to write a full perfectly working example of HopChar2 for you, sorry.",
